    {
        "context": "concept_explain", "topic_area": "ml",
        "prompt": "I keep hearing 'overfitting' — what does it actually mean?",
        "rejected": "## Overfitting Explained 📊\n\n### Definition:\nWhen a model performs well on training data but poorly on new, unseen data.\n\n### Why It Happens:\n- Model memorizes training data\n- Captures noise, not signal\n- Too complex for the dataset\n\n### How to Detect:\n| Metric | Training | Validation |\n|--------|----------|------------|\n| Loss | Low | High |\n| Accuracy | High | Low |\n| Gap | Large = overfitting |\n\n### Common Causes:\n- 🔴 Model too large for data\n- 🔴 Too many epochs of training\n- 🔴 Lack of regularization\n- 🔴 Imbalanced/small dataset\n\n### How to Prevent:\n\n**1. Regularization:**\n- L1/L2 weight penalties\n- Dropout\n- Early stopping\n\n**2. More Data:**\n- Collect more samples\n- Data augmentation\n\n**3. Simpler Model:**\n- Fewer parameters\n- Reduce model capacity\n\n**4. Cross-Validation:**\n- K-fold cross-validation\n- Holdout validation set\n\n### Visual Indicator:\n- Training loss continues decreasing\n- Validation loss starts increasing\n- The gap between them grows\n\n### Verdict:\nManaging overfitting is one of the most important skills in ML!",
        "chosen": "Overfitting is what happens when your model basically memorizes the training data instead of learning the pattern behind it.\n\nImagine you're teaching a kid to recognize cats by showing them a hundred photos. If you keep drilling them on those exact same hundred photos, eventually they'll get 100% accuracy — but only on those photos. Show them a new cat they've never seen and they're stumped. They didn't learn what makes a cat a cat; they memorized 'photo 47 is a cat, photo 48 is a dog.' That's overfitting.\n\nIn ML, you'll see this as a model that scores beautifully on the training data but flops the moment you test it on data it hasn't seen. The classic sign is when your training loss keeps going down while your validation loss starts creeping back up — at that point the model is no longer learning, it's just memorizing.\n\nThe usual fixes are: get more data so memorization is harder, make the model simpler so it has less capacity to memorize, or use techniques like dropout and early stopping that explicitly discourage memorization. For your RL project, the equivalent risk is the model overfitting to your specific training prompts and not generalizing — which is why we'll keep a clean held-out test set."
    },
